1. Parties, software and scope

1.1 Licensor. “Licensor” means Adam Maddox and a lawful successor to the relevant rights and obligations. Control of a username, domain, repository or organization does not by itself confer ownership or authority to grant permissions. The identifiers above are contact and identification references, not additional licensors.

1.2 Covered Release and Covered Material. A “Covered Release” is a source revision or binary release to which Licensor expressly applies this exact license version by an accompanying licensing notice. “Covered Material” means the protectable project code, documentation and other material in that release that Licensor owns or has authority to license under these terms. Covered Material retains that identity when copied or incorporated into a Modification; this definition does not authorize an otherwise unauthorized copy, use or distribution. Separately licensed third-party material is excluded and remains subject to its own terms. Historical revisions are included only when expressly identified in a Licensor-issued licensing-scope record. This license does not retrospectively narrow an existing grant or create copyright in otherwise unprotectable material.

1.3 Product names. “ChezCat,” “ChezCat Browser,” “chezcat browser,” and “chezcat-browser,” including capitalization, spacing and hyphenation variants, identify the same project when used in that context. A rename or rebrand does not remove obligations attached to Covered Material. Similarity of name alone does not bring an unrelated project under this license.

1.4 You and organizations. “You” means the individual or legal entity relying on these permissions. An individual accepting on behalf of an organization must have authority to do so. An “Organization” is a business, sole proprietorship, nonprofit or other legal entity acting for its own operations. Its “Internal Users” are (a) its employees and its owners, partners, members, officers and directors; (b) individual contractors, temporary or leased workers, interns and volunteers while acting solely for that Organization's internal activities on systems or accounts the Organization controls or authorizes; and (c) IT contractors acting within Section 3.3. A customer, vendor, separate business partner or separate affiliate does not become an Internal User merely through that relationship. An IT provider supplying services as a contractor must satisfy Section 3.3 and cannot use category (b) to avoid its engagement-specific restrictions. Each separate affiliated Organization must rely on its own acceptance and permissions. This license does not add an automatic distribution grant for students or other external recipients merely because of their relationship with an Organization.

1.5 Modifications and Modified Builds. A “Modification” alters, adapts, translates, ports or incorporates protectable Covered Material. A “Modified Build” is a source or executable version containing a Modification. Changing programming language or claiming independent authorship does not make copied or adapted expression independent. A genuinely independently authored implementation that does not incorporate or adapt protectable Covered Material is not a Modification merely because it implements the same public standard, uses the same general idea or interoperates with ChezCat. This license asserts no exclusive rights over ideas, facts, public standards or material outside Licensor's rights.

1.6 Copies, Hosted Access and demonstrations. “Supplying Copies” includes providing source or binaries to another person by download, installer, mirror, package, bundle, transfer or other distribution. “Hosted Access” means enabling someone outside Your permitted Personal Use or Internal Use to operate ChezCat remotely or consume its browser or engine functionality as a service or API, including a service that performs browser automation, rendering or screenshot jobs for outside recipients. It can be public, invitation-only, partner, vendor or customer access; a fee or direct remote-control connection is not required.

Showing screenshots, a recording, or a presenter-operated demonstration under Section 4.2 is not Hosted Access when the audience does not receive an interactive browser service. Questions or requests that a presenter voluntarily demonstrates during an explanation do not alone create Hosted Access. Giving the audience remote input, an API, a job queue or equivalent control of browser functionality is different. Nor may an operator label a rendering or automation service a “demonstration” merely because a human relays customers' commands. Ordinary reports or screenshots incidental to permitted internal work are not, by themselves, a hosted-browser service.

1.7 Written Approval. “Written Approval” means an express, authenticated written permission from Licensor or an expressly authorized representative identifying the permitted activity and its scope. A signed agreement or authenticated approval in the official repository may document it. Silence, an issue being opened, an unrelated pull request being merged, an automated acknowledgement, or an endorsement request does not constitute approval. An exceptional commercial agreement must expressly identify the commercial rights it grants. Written Approvals do not silently extend to another recipient, release or activity.

Except where this license or an applicable separate agreement expressly states a different standard, Licensor may grant, condition or refuse a new Written Approval, endorsement, hosting approval, commercial permission or discretionary reinstatement in Licensor's sole discretion, subject to nonwaivable law. No approval is owed merely because another applicant obtained one. This discretion does not displace Section 11.2's good-faith and no-unreasonable-refusal standard for qualifying reinstatement requests or enlarge the rights that a particular approval may grant.

For an approval issued under this Version 1.1, unless its express terms or a separate agreement provide otherwise, Licensor may withdraw the special approval prospectively by written notice taking effect no earlier than 30 days after established receipt under Section 15.2. Licensor may suspend its affected special permissions immediately by written notice where reasonably necessary to address a material breach of its conditions, material misrepresentation, or a serious security or misleading-endorsement risk. The notice must identify the approval, affected permissions, reason and effective time. Contractual or legal rights to terminate for breach, expiry or an express approval condition remain separate. This paragraph does not retroactively change an approval issued under earlier terms, override an express separately negotiated commitment, revoke a compliant recipient's existing base-license copyright or patent grant, or make ordinary permitted use depend on continued endorsement.

1.8 Personal Use and Internal Use. “Personal Use” is an individual's use of ChezCat for that individual's own private purposes under Section 2, including private modification, compilation, testing and automation. “Internal Use” is an Organization's operation, deployment, customization, testing and automation of ChezCat for its own internal activities through its Internal Users under Section 3. Ordinary paid work is included. Neither term includes Supplying Copies or providing Hosted Access beyond the specific permissions in this license. A separately licensed or legally permitted activity remains governed by Section 9.

1.9 Commercial offerings. An “Independent ChezCat Commercial Offering” is a separately marketed or supplied product, subscription, installation, customization, support or other offering whose subject is ChezCat or its browser/engine functionality, rather than a general IT engagement within Section 3.3. A derivative product in Section 6 is a product incorporating or adapting Covered Material, not a wholly independent implementation within Section 1.5. Charges, subscriptions, advertising or indirect financial benefits can identify a commercial offering even when the program itself is advertised as free.

1.10 Title. The title's reference to “Personal Use” does not narrow the Internal Use and Organization permissions in Section 3 or any other operative permission in this license.

2. Personal use, source review and private changes

Subject to the scope and conditions of this license, Licensor grants You a worldwide, nonexclusive, royalty-free copyright license, of the duration stated in Section 12.1, to:

(a) Obtain, install, run and make reasonable private copies and backups of Covered Releases for Your Personal Use.

(b) Inspect, study, audit, test and debug Covered Material. This permission does not depend on Your occupation or on whether Your employer permits research during working hours.

(c) Modify, compile and run Modified Builds for Your Personal Use, including changes made simply because You prefer different behavior. Modification and compilation are not limited to inspection, study or audit.

(d) Keep private development history, backups and builds in access-controlled storage for permitted activities. This is not permission to publish a mirror or make a generally downloadable build.

(e) Access Your own installation remotely for Personal Use and operate it through scripts, headless mode or test harnesses for Your own permitted tasks, without providing Hosted Access to others.

You need not submit Your private changes to Licensor. Making Modifications does not itself assign ownership of Your newly authored material; an accepted contribution requires Section 8's separate process. Except for specific permissions elsewhere in this license, this section does not allow incorporating Covered Material into a different product, even one used only internally. Customizing ChezCat as a browser and externally driving it with a permitted script or harness do not, merely by those acts, incorporate its implementation into another product.

These permissions do not grant authority over third-party websites, services, accounts or data. Obtain any separate authorization required for the systems or information You access.

3. Ordinary workplace and organizational use

Licensor grants each Organization a worldwide, nonexclusive, royalty-free copyright license, of the duration stated in Section 12.1, to exercise the permissions in this Section 3, subject to their scope and conditions.

3.1 Internal operation and automation. An Organization may use official builds or Modified Builds for Internal Use, including ordinary workplace browsing and paid work. It may install, update, modify, compile and back up those builds, keep access-controlled internal installer or source repositories, and provide remote browser access solely to its Internal Users. It may run ChezCat through scripts, headless mode or test harnesses for its own internal QA, testing and other internal tasks. Connecting a test harness to the browser is not, by itself, prohibited incorporation of Covered Material into a different product.

Ordinary business productivity, salary and revenue from an Organization's underlying work do not by themselves commercialize ChezCat. This permission does not cover offering ChezCat-powered rendering, browser automation, screenshots or equivalent browser functionality to customers or other outsiders as a service, regardless of whether a human employee or an internal API initiates the work. Ordinary reports and incidental screenshots produced in internal work, and demonstrations permitted by Section 4, are not prohibited merely because they are later discussed with others.

3.2 Internal distribution. Supplying Copies solely to Internal Users described in Section 1.4(a) and (b) for that Organization's Internal Use is permitted. IT contractors acting as service providers may receive necessary copies only through Section 3.3. Internal Modified Builds must meet Section 5's notice and labeling requirements. This does not permit Supplying Copies to customers, bundling ChezCat with a product supplied to others, presenting the base browser as the Organization's creation, or supplying builds to separate entities merely because they are affiliates, vendors or partners.

3.3 Paid internal contractors. An Organization may pay an IT contractor to install, maintain, audit or customize ChezCat solely on that Organization's behalf for its permitted Internal Use. Before providing access or copies, it must obtain the contractor's own affirmative acceptance of this license for the engagement, including its applicable scope, attribution and access restrictions. The contractor must be authorized to accept for any entity providing the service. The contractor's permissions extend only to engagements within this Section 3.3, not to an engagement obtained or performed through an Independent ChezCat Commercial Offering made without Written Approval. Failure to obtain that agreement is a failure of this permission's conditions. The Organization must not authorize or knowingly facilitate activities outside this exception. This license does not automatically impute every independent contractor act to the Organization or impose an undisclosed indemnity.

The contractor may receive necessary copies, create project-specific Modifications, and return work to that Organization. Permission for the engagement is specific to that Organization. It does not authorize carrying a ChezCat build or Modification prepared for one client into another client's deployment, selling a ChezCat-based product, or offering Hosted Access. Each additional engagement needs its own applicable authorization; this is not a reseller or multi-customer distribution license. An Organization may arrange an authorized download from an official source for its own engagement.

On completion, copies of Covered Material and Modifications retained solely for that engagement must be returned or removed. Necessary legal, security or engagement records may be retained in access-controlled archives solely for recordkeeping, not used for further deployment, development for other clients or commercial services. Retained source, binaries or patches must be limited in scope to what is reasonably necessary to document the engagement, address a security matter or satisfy a legal-preservation need, including any applicable legal-preservation duty, and may be retained only for as long as reasonably necessary for that purpose. When that need ends, the Organization or contractor holding the material must return or delete it. Access throughout retention remains confined to the permitted recordkeeping purpose. Retention is not a right to maintain a reusable deployment inventory or resume an ended engagement. A contractor's independently authored, separable tools, scripts, configurations, know-how or code that contain no protectable Covered Material are not assigned to Licensor or automatically forbidden from reuse by this clause. Ownership between the contractor and client must be established by their own agreement; CSAPUL does not decide it.

This exception does not authorize advertising or operating an Independent ChezCat Commercial Offering. A general IT provider may accept a client's internal-administration request and charge its normal fees without an additional license merely because the work involves ChezCat. Advertising a ChezCat-specific service line, subscription or standard customized browser package requires separate Written Approval under Section 6(d). The number of unrelated general IT clients alone does not establish such an offering; use of a common ChezCat-based product or prohibited cross-client distribution remains outside the exception. Factually listing ChezCat among the software a general IT provider supports is not, by itself, an Independent ChezCat Commercial Offering and does not imply endorsement.

4. Discussion, excerpts and demonstrations

4.1 Findings and limited excerpts. You may report bugs, propose improvements, and publish truthful criticism, reviews, security analysis and teaching material. You may include attributed excerpts or small explanatory diffs reasonably necessary to explain the particular issue or lesson. No excerpt may exceed what is reasonably necessary to explain the particular issue or lesson. This does not authorize distributing substantial ready-to-reuse components, complete builds, or excerpts that collectively reconstruct a substantial part of a file, component or release, whether in one publication or a series. There is no fixed line-count allowance. It does not restrict rights You independently have under applicable law.

4.2 Public and monetized demonstrations. You may show official or permitted modified builds through screenshots, videos, livestreams, reviews, lectures, presentations and courses, including monetized content. Identify ChezCat and distinguish Your modifications from the official product. This permission covers showing and discussing the program, not providing downloadable software or interactive Hosted Access to the audience. It is not a permission to sell customized installations or a support subscription.

4.3 Independent expression and data. Licensor does not acquire rights in Your reviews, ordinary browsing output, reports or teaching material merely because they refer to ChezCat. Feedback deliberately submitted to the project is treated separately by Section 8. You must have independent authority for any third-party content or personal data You include in a report or demonstration.

5. Attribution, modifications and branding

Retain the applicable copyright, license and third-party notices in authorized source and binary copies. Do not falsely claim authorship or ownership of the base project, remove its required notices, or imply that Licensor endorses Your work when no such approval exists. You may truthfully identify Your own contributions and changes.

A Modified Build shown to others or supplied under an authorized exception must be clearly identified as “Unofficial modified build”, identify its maintainer, and make a concise description of material changes available. A clear About/build-information notice and accompanying change record are sufficient; an elaborate banner in every source file is not required. For a demonstration under Section 4.2, a clear caption, on-screen notice or spoken disclosure identifying the unofficial modified build and its maintainer is sufficient; an overlay in every frame is not required. Accurate source-file notices and Git authorship must not be falsified.

The branding permission in this section is limited to the uses and labeling stated here and is not official endorsement. Subject to those conditions, You may retain ChezCat's name and official project logo in permitted modified builds and their demonstrations, to the extent Licensor controls the necessary rights. You may make factual references and reproduce the logo as part of permitted screenshots or reviews. This is not a general trademark license for unrelated products, merchandising or misleading commercial promotion, and it does not represent that any name or logo is registered.

Licensor may publish reasonable quality, security and labeling standards for the permitted use of the ChezCat name and logo on Modified Builds. Licensor may require by written notice that a specific Modified Build stop using the name or logo if it fails those standards or could mislead users about its source, quality or security. Compliance with that notice is a condition of this branding permission. These standards govern branding, not an expansion or revocation of an otherwise valid software copyright or patent grant. Accurate attribution, historical identification and independently lawful descriptive references remain permitted; required attribution must not be removed to imply authorship of the base project.

No freestanding right to distribute branded builds is created by retaining a logo. Official endorsement and approved branding for a branch require the version-specific Written Approval described in Section 7. Attribution does not create ownership, approval authority, or a right to have contributed code retained indefinitely.

6. Restricted commercialization and copying

Except to the extent expressly permitted by Sections 2–5 and 8, an approval under Section 7, independently applicable rights preserved by Section 9, or a separately negotiated Written Approval expressly granting the necessary rights, You may not:

(a) Sell, rent, commercially license or otherwise supply the browser or a derivative product for compensation; sublicense rights in Covered Material that You have not been granted; or bundle it with products supplied to customers, including products presented as Your own.

(b) Copy or adapt Covered Material into another product, library, engine, SDK, service or rebranded browser. Implementing independent ideas or standards without using protectable Covered Material remains outside this restriction.

(c) Make public source or binary distributions, mirrors, installers, modified builds or derivative products, except the specific platform, contribution, excerpt and approved-branch permissions recognized by this license.

(d) Advertise, offer or sell ChezCat-specific support subscriptions, customization, installation or comparable services as an Independent ChezCat Commercial Offering (Section 1.9) without Written Approval. The paid internal-contractor exception in Section 3.3 and monetized discussion/demonstration permission in Section 4.2 control over this restriction where they apply.

(e) Provide Hosted Access to any person outside Your permitted Personal Use or Internal Use without prior Written Approval and compliance with Section 7.2, including private, invitation-only, partner, vendor, customer or public access.

No general right to assign, sublicense or transfer these permissions to another recipient is granted. An authorized employee or contractor acting within Section 3 is not an assignment of the license. Each other recipient must have its own valid grant under this license, an approval or independent rights. This does not restrict transfers independently protected by law or an existing grant.

Calling software or access “free,” charging for surrounding services instead of the binary, or renaming the software does not enlarge these permissions. These restrictions concern exploitation of ChezCat itself, not ordinary business browsing or other expressly permitted activities.

7. Endorsed branches and public hosting

7.1 Endorsed distributions. You may request designation as an officially endorsed branch through the official repository's endorsement-request channel. Only Written Approval identifying the maintainer, versions or exact revisions, distribution channels, branding and applicable conditions grants that status. An approval may permit free public source and binary distributions within that stated scope, with attribution and the specified CSAPUL terms preserved. It does not automatically cover later modifications, sales, commercial bundling or Hosted Access.

Quality, security, maintenance, review and suspension/withdrawal requirements must be stated in the approval. The official endorsement register records approvals and their status; it does not create permissions absent the written grant. Ending endorsement does not, by itself, terminate compliant recipients' previously granted use permissions for an approved release. It does end any permission to imply ongoing endorsement beyond the approval's scope or duration. Historical claims of endorsement must identify the version and period accurately.

7.2 Hosted services. Any Hosted Access requires prior Written Approval even when it is free, private, invitation-only or limited to partners or vendors. An approval under this section is available only for access that produces no direct or indirect monetary or monetary-equivalent benefit associated with offering, operating or promoting that service. This excludes access charges, subscriptions, paid-tier tie-ins, advertising, linked sponsorship or fundraising, affiliate commissions, selling or monetizing user data, paid lead generation, and using the hosted service to promote paid offerings. Passing proceeds to someone else or describing them as cost recovery does not itself create an exception.

Ordinary unrelated workplace revenue under Section 3 and monetized reviews/demonstrations under Section 4 are not Hosted Access. A GitHub-hosted repository is governed by the distinct rights in Section 9; this section does not attempt to withdraw GitHub's applicable platform rights.

An endorsement, a free-hosting request or permission under this section is not a commercial-hosting license. Any future commercial deal must be separately and expressly negotiated; no such rights are granted by this license. Hosting approval does not automatically authorize distribution of copies or official branding.

8. Feedback and contributions

8.1 Non-code feedback. For ideas, suggestions and bug reports You voluntarily submit to the official project as feedback, You permit Licensor and lawful successors to act on the ideas and reported facts to develop, change and commercialize products without payment, further approval or an obligation to implement them. This is not ownership of the ideas or an unrestricted license to sell Your protected explanatory text as a separate work. To the extent You own relevant copyright in explanatory text, You grant a nonexclusive, worldwide, royalty-free license to reproduce, edit and quote that text as reasonably needed for evaluation, issue handling and project documentation, and to transfer or sublicense that permission as part of maintaining or transferring the project.

This feedback permission continues for the applicable copyright term and survives termination of Your software-use permission, subject to nonwaivable law. It does not assign ownership of ideas, transfer patents, override third-party rights, or authorize disclosure of private security details, credentials or personal information contrary to applicable confidentiality and privacy obligations. It does not promise a bounty, credit or compensation for ordinary feedback.

Original minimal reproducer code deliberately supplied to the official project to demonstrate a report may be copied, executed and adapted solely to reproduce and evaluate that report, subject to the submitter having the necessary rights and applicable confidentiality. This limited evaluation permission does not assign its copyright or authorize incorporating it in a shipped product or maintained test suite without Section 8.2's inclusion process.

8.2 Proposed material for inclusion. Code patches, substantial test implementations, artwork and documentation submitted for inclusion are not automatically treated as non-code feedback. Contributions must follow the separately signed ChezCat Contributor Copyright Assignment and Patent Agreement and its acceptance record. The project may accept, reject, request changes, edit, remove or decline to use a submission. Submitting or merging a pull request is not, by itself, a copyright assignment.

You may provide the official project with the limited Covered Material context reasonably needed to propose or review a patch. That permission does not authorize publishing a separate derivative product. Obtain and retain an explicit submission-evaluation permission and a rights/identity check before copying, executing or modifying substantive material for evaluation beyond independently granted platform rights. The evaluation record must identify the accepted evaluation terms, the Submission's immutable revision or digest, the assenting rights holder or authorized representative, and the acknowledgement date. Merely opening a pull request is not treated as recorded assent to C2. The separate signed assignment and exact acceptance must precede incorporation in the maintained product or test-suite history; a pull-request branch offered for review is not itself acceptance. Public pull requests are public, can remain in forks or platform records after rejection, and are subject to GitHub's applicable inbound licensing and other terms. Do not submit confidential material publicly; use a separately agreed private route. Rejection ends any prospect of assignment unless separately agreed, but does not undo rights already granted to a platform or its users. The assignment addresses the limited license-back of the contributor's own material and the separate patent grant.

8.2A Contribution forks. Solely to propose or support evaluation of a Submission to the official project, You may fork the official repository on its official hosting platform, make Modifications in that fork, and keep its source publicly visible there. The fork may remain after acceptance, rejection or withdrawal as a historical source record of that contribution activity; this does not permit developing or presenting it as an alternative distributed product. Section 5's applicable attribution, maintainer, modification-summary and unofficial-status notices must appear in its README or equivalent accompanying notice.

This additional permission covers source in that on-platform contribution fork only. It does not authorize off-platform public distribution or mirrors, platform release binaries or other packaged distributions, package-registry publication, Hosted Access, or an Independent ChezCat Commercial Offering. Private clones, builds and tests otherwise authorized by Sections 2 or 3 remain permitted; “off-platform distribution” here does not prohibit those separately authorized private activities. This permission does not assign Your contribution to Licensor, waive Section 8.2's evaluation and signed-inclusion process, or restrict any permission independently granted by applicable platform terms or law.

8.3 Security reporting. Use the verified private reporting route described in the official SECURITY.md for sensitive findings, and public issues for ordinary bugs or suggestions. This is a reporting preference, not an indefinite gag clause or waiver of independent legal rights. This license grants no authority to attack third-party systems or disclose others' secrets.

9. Independent legal, platform and third-party rights

This license does not reduce rights You possess independently under applicable law, an existing valid license, or binding permissions actually granted through a hosting platform. Applicable GitHub viewing, on-platform forking, contribution and other platform permissions remain unaffected. Permissions granted to GitHub and its affiliates under applicable platform terms are separate from permissions granted by this license to other recipients. This acknowledgment is not a new unrestricted off-platform distribution, commercialization or general AI-training license for everyone.

The extent of a platform permission depends on the terms actually applicable to the content and parties. A platform feature's technical availability does not itself establish every legal right to use it. This license neither adds a blanket exception for fork Releases, mirrors or hosted services nor overrides a right the platform terms independently grant. Deleting or hiding a repository, release or notice does not necessarily retrieve existing forks or terminate permissions already granted to their recipients. No universal recall is promised.

Contributions made through a platform can also carry inbound licensing under that platform's terms. Such permissions are not a copyright assignment and do not replace ChezCat's signed acceptance process. A separate executed contribution agreement controls its subject to the extent legally effective; merely referring to an unsigned agreement does not execute it.

Third-party code, data, fonts, certificates, artwork, runtime components and other separately licensed material keep their own terms and required notices. Their inclusion does not make Licensor their owner. Where a prior grant permits broader use of identified material, these terms do not purport to cancel it. Those independent rights do not automatically extend to the rest of ChezCat. Nothing here represents that every line or generated artifact is independently copyrightable.

10. Limited patent permission

10.1 Licensor-controlled claims. For activities otherwise permitted by this license or specific Written Approval, Licensor grants You a nonexclusive, worldwide, royalty-free patent license to make, have made, compile, run, use, test, modify and import authorized copies and Modifications, and to perform transfers or other activities only to the extent separately authorized. The grant covers only patent claims Licensor owns or controls now or later, has authority to license, and that are necessarily practiced by a Covered Release as supplied or an accepted contribution alone or in its supplied combination with that release. For a successor Licensor, this grant covers only relevant claims acquired from or through Adam Maddox, together with claims the successor expressly elects in writing to license under this Section. The limitation does not withdraw an already-effective grant for a Covered Release. No current patent ownership is represented. This patent license continues for the full term of the licensed claims and is irrevocable except as provided in Section 10.3 or as nonwaivable law requires; its authorized-activity scope remains applicable.

For permitted Modifications, the grant continues to cover those licensed claims; it does not extend to additional claims infringed solely because You introduce new material or unrelated combinations. The grant gives no additional copyright, distribution, hosting or commercialization permission and transfers no patent ownership. Claims available to Licensor solely through an executed contributor patent agreement are addressed in Section 10.2 and that agreement, not treated as Licensor-owned claims under this subsection. This distinction does not enlarge or withdraw an independently executed grant or an authorized sublicense.

10.2 Contributor claims. Accepted contributions may have additional patent grants under their executed Contributor Copyright Assignment and Patent Agreement. Under C7.2 of that agreement, authorized recipients are direct grantees and intended beneficiaries of the specified downstream patent permission, limited to their authorized activities. This includes ordinary recipients whose use of the contribution in a Covered Release is authorized by CSAPUL, without an individual approval, as well as recipients authorized by a specific Written Approval or other applicable project license. That permission must actually have been granted by a person with authority; this license does not invent grants from unsigned agreements or from unrelated patent holders. Licensor will retain the relevant executed records and make a redacted confirmation of the applicable grant available to an authorized recipient on reasonable request without unnecessary disclosure of personal information.

10.3 Patent litigation. If You initiate or direct patent litigation, including a counterclaim or cross-claim, alleging that ChezCat or a contribution incorporated in it infringes a patent claim You own or control, Your patent license granted under Section 10.1 terminates upon filing. A contributor's direct grant is governed by the matching trigger in its executed C7.3. The consequence applies to the litigating grantee's patent permission, not automatically to its copyright permissions or to other grantees. No automatic penalty is imposed on an affiliate merely because of affiliation. Unrelated disputes, criticism, security reports and bug reports do not trigger this clause. Reinstatement requires an express written grant by the relevant patent licensor, not Section 11's ordinary correction process.

11. Breach, termination and correction

11.1 Scope, conditions and other obligations. The permissions to copy, modify, compile, distribute and demonstrate Covered Material are limited to the permitted purposes, recipients and approval exceptions in this license and are conditioned on compliance with their applicable notice, attribution, scope and copying/distribution requirements. Activity outside a grant's scope is not authorized by that grant. Breaching a condition of the copyright permissions terminates Your copyright permissions under this license, subject to applicable law and reinstatement below. Calling an obligation a condition does not decide whether a particular act is copyright infringement; that determination remains governed by applicable law. Other enforceable contractual obligations and the separate patent and branding permissions have their own operation. This section does not terminate rights independently preserved by Section 9.

Upon discovering a violation or receiving notice, stop the unauthorized conduct immediately. A correction period does not authorize continued sales, distribution, hosting or other prohibited conduct. You may retain and use only controlled copies strictly necessary to correct the violation or meet legal preservation duties, not for ordinary operation under terminated permissions.

11.2 First unintentional violation and correction. For a first unintentional violation, Your copyright permissions automatically reinstate when You fully correct the violation after receipt of Licensor's first written notice identifying it and no later than 30 days after that receipt. Notice and receipt are addressed in Section 15.2. If all required correction was already complete when the notice is received and the other requirements of this section are met, reinstatement under this notice-based process occurs upon that receipt, not before it.

For this correction process, an unintentional violation includes accidental noncompliance and a violation caused by a genuine, reasonable, good-faith misunderstanding of this license. Intentionally performing an act is not, by itself, knowingly violating the license. Reasonableness and good faith depend on the actual circumstances, the applicable terms, and available evidence; merely asserting a mistaken belief does not establish them. This classification affects eligibility for reinstatement, not whether the underlying conduct was authorized. Section 11.3's deliberate and repeated violations do not qualify.

Full correction includes restoring notices, ending unauthorized offers or access, and removing copies from channels under Your control. For copies already supplied, make reasonable documented efforts to notify recipients of the unauthorized distribution, request removal or cessation as applicable, and stop continuing distribution You can control. No impossible recall of every internet copy is required. Section 11.1's immediate-cessation requirement applies throughout.

Correction before receipt of a written notice does not, by itself, automatically reinstate permissions. Stop promptly, complete correction and request express written reinstatement; do not wait for notice before correcting the problem. Licensor will assess in good faith a request establishing a first unintentional violation that has been fully corrected and will not unreasonably refuse reinstatement of a qualifying request. Licensor may request reasonable information needed to assess the violation and correction. Deliberate or repeated conduct, incomplete correction or materially misleading information does not qualify for that protection. Until express written reinstatement or reinstatement through the notice-based process above, terminated permissions remain terminated. Silence, elapsed time, submission of a request or acknowledgement of its receipt is not approval. No reinstatement predates the applicable written approval or qualifying notice-based event. Licensor will respond within a reasonable time to a request that appears to qualify after receiving the information reasonably requested. This does not make silence or delay an approval or authorize ordinary use while permissions remain terminated.

11.3 Deliberate, repeated and other nonqualifying violations. A deliberate violation is conduct undertaken with actual knowledge that it exceeds an applicable permission or breaches an applicable condition. Knowing concealment, intentional removal of notices to obscure ownership, or continuing conduct known to be prohibited after clear notice can evidence that knowledge. A genuine, reasonable, good-faith misunderstanding described in Section 11.2 is not deliberate merely because the person intended the underlying act. Commercial conduct is not presumed innocent merely because the operator labels it free, and a disagreement about classification is not conclusively resolved by either party's label.

A repeated violation is a further distinct violation after receipt of written notice of a prior violation or after reinstatement following an earlier violation. Continuing incidents within the original unresolved violation are not separate reinstatements. There is no automatic two- or five-year reset.

Deliberate violations, including deliberate unauthorized commercialization or false authorship claims, and repeated violations are ineligible for automatic reinstatement. Any violation that does not qualify for reinstatement under Section 11.2, including a nonqualifying misunderstanding or correction outside the notice-based 30-day period, requires Licensor's express written approval. The good-faith and no-unreasonable-refusal standard in Section 11.2 applies only to its qualifying pre-notice correction requests, not to deliberate, repeated or other nonqualifying violations. Correction may still be required and does not guarantee reinstatement. None of these rules alters Section 11.4's preservation of claims for prior conduct.

11.4 Limits of reinstatement. Reinstatement is prospective. It does not waive claims for prior conduct, erase accrued obligations, restore endorsement or hosting approvals, or restore a patent permission terminated for patent litigation. A distributor's breach does not by itself terminate permissions validly granted to compliant recipients. Attribution, applicable restrictions, disclaimers, dispute provisions and accrued obligations survive as necessary to give them effect, subject to applicable law.

